Google for 'Cantor', 'set theory', 'countability'.  
The story in short:  Assume you could assign in a unique 
way a natural number to each real number between 0 and 1, 
then the reals could be listed as x^{1}, x^{2}, .. etc.  
Consider the binary extension of each x^{j}, say, 

  x^{j} = 0.x^{j}_1 x^{j}_2.., 

and build now a new real number *x by choosing, for each j, 
the j--th part of *x's binary expansion to be the opposite 
of x^{j}'s j--th part.  Then *x is none of the x^{j}'s, since 
it differs in at least one part of its binary expansion from 
every x^{j}, and hence the list of elements of [0,1] is not 
complete.  

Of course, there are some technical details left out (binary 
expansion not unique, etc.), so here is a longer and slightly 
more formal version:  

We shall say that a mapping f, mapping some set A into some 
other set B, is injective if two distinct elements a, a' of 
A are mapped onto distinct elements f(a), f(a') in B.  The 
set A is said to be /of greater cardinality/ than B if such 
an injective mapping f from A to B does _not_ exist.  

Example:  A = {1, .., 5}, B = {1, .., 6}, 
then the mapping f(i) = i is injective from 
A to B.  However, for A = {1, .., 5} and 
B = {1, .., 4} there is no injective mapping 
from A to B, hence A is of greater cardinality 
than B.  

We say that A and B have the same cardinality 
if neither is of greater cardinality than the 
other one.  It is a non--trivial result that 
this is the case if and only if there exists 
a mapping f from A to B such that, for each b 
in B, there is exactly one a in A with f(a) = b.  
(One can imagine that b and a are 'identified' 
via f; if you know either of the pair, you know 
the other one as well).  Such mappings are called 
/bijective/.  

