Okay, back to the blackboard.  The true power 
of this notion of cardinality does not lie in 
the study of finite sets (where it coincides 
with the usual 'counting' method), but in the 
study of infinite ones.  While many sets have 
the same cardinality (e.g., the set of natural 
numbers, the set of integers, the set of rational 
numbers), it turns out that there are also infinite 
sets of different cardinality.  The simplest example 
is the set of natural numbers and the set of real 
numbers.  For this we need a technical lemma.  Let 
|N be the set of natural numbers.  

Lemma:  If A is an infinite set, then 
A and A u |N are of the same cardinality; 
in other words, there is an injective mapping 
f from A u |N into A.  

Taking this lemma for granted, we can prove:  

Claim:  The set [0,1] of real numbers between 
0 and 1 is of greater cardinality than the 
set of natural numbers. 

Proof:  First we observe that [0,1] is, 
due to the above lemma, equal in cardinality 
to the set of sequences (x_1, x_2, ..) with 
x_i \in {0,1}.  This is because, if we exclude 
sequences such that x_i becomes constantly 1 
after some time, the mapping 

f(x_1, x_2, ..) := \sum_{j=1}^{oo} 2^{-i} * x_i  

is a bijective mapping onto [0,1]; since the set 
we excluded is of the same cardinality than |N, 
we can easily conclude that the set of all such 
sequences is of the same cardinality than [0,1].  
Hence we just need to deal with the set of all 
sequences (x_1, x_2, ..).  Assume now that this 
set of sequences would not be of greater cardinality 
than |N; then there would be an injective map 
f from this set into |N.  Denote x^{j} to be the 
sequence (x^{j}_1, x^{j}_2, ..) such that 
f(x^{j}) = j, if such an element exists at all; 
else we set x^{j} := (0,0,...).  For y \in {0,1}, 
set *y its 'dual', i.e., *y is 0 iff y is 1 and 
*y is 1 iff y is 0.  Consider now the sequence  
*x := (*x^{1}_1, *x^{2}_2,..); then f(*x) must be 
some natural number, say, f(*x) = *j.  From the 
definition of x^{j}, it follows that *x = x^{j}; 
however, this is impossible since the j-th 
entry in *x is *x^{j}_j, the exact opposite 
of x^{j}_j, which is the j-th entry in x^{j}.  
The contradiction shows our assumption was 
wrong, hence the claim is correct.  

Of course, all depends on wether you accept our 
notion of cardinality to be the 'correct' one.  

